{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>בְּמַרְאוֹת הַצּוֹבְאוֹת</b> — the daughters of Israel had mirrors in their possession which they looked into when adorning themselves, and even these they did not withhold. But Moses found them repugnant because they are made for the evil inclination. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: These are more beloved than all, for through them the women raised up multitudes in Egypt, etc. And let the basin be made from them, for it serves to make peace between a man and his wife — to give drink from its waters to a woman whose husband has warned her and who has secluded herself. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 38:8).\n\nThe rationale in this midrash (Tanhuma Pekudei 9) is that for all the work of the Tabernacle they accepted ornaments from the women, as it is written (Exodus 35:22), \"And the men came upon the women,\" and they brought brooches and earrings and rings and kumaz, and the kumaz according to its midrashic interpretation (in tractate Shabbat 64a) is even more repugnant. But there, all the donation was mixed together; whereas to make a designated vessel from an ornament fashioned for the evil inclination — Moses would not have chosen such a thing until he was so instructed from the mouth of the Almighty.\n\nAnd I do not know how, according to this, \"אֲשֶׁר צָבְאוּ פֶּתַח אֹהֶל מוֹעֵד\" is to be interpreted. Perhaps one may say that the women brought that donation to the tent of Moses, which was called the Tent of Meeting, and he himself accepted it from them by divine speech, for the Tabernacle itself had not yet been made.\n\nOnkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 38:8) translated: \"who came to pray at the entrance of the tent of appointed time\" — his words lean toward the words of R. Eliezer, who said that these women were servants of HaShem, and had withdrawn from the desires of this world, and gave their mirrors as a donation, and came every single day to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting to pray and to hear the words of the commandments.\n\nIt is also possible, according to the plain sense, to say that he made the basin and its base from the mirrors of the women through whom a great multitude was raised up, and who assembled at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting to give their mirrors as a freewill offering. And the copper of the mirrors was light, polished copper, exceedingly beautiful, and therefore he designated it from the outset for this vessel. And when the women saw this, they assembled and came, troop by troop, to give all their mirrors, so that the entire basin and its base would be made from them. And it is also correct that from the outset they intended this because of the matter of the sotah, and they accepted this upon themselves with joy and volunteered to give all their mirrors for it."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Rashi's midrash (from Tanhuma Pekudei 9 and b. Shabbat 64a) attributing the mirrors' repugnance to Moses; the phrase 'the Holy One, blessed be He' translates הקב\"ה literally. R. Eliezer's position is referenced via Onkelos without a precise source citation. The phrase 'troop by troop' renders צְבָאוֹת צְבָאוֹת, echoing the verse's root צ-ב-א."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:22:19.529862004Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}